Chess players employ a variety of opening strategies, which can be analyzed through a technique called chessboard representation. This method entails calculating a crucial index, the Kings' Defense Perimeter (KDP), for distinct opening strategies. By applying intricate data analysis to a large chess community dataset (comprising ~880,000 chess games), researchers identified both simple and complex categorizations of opening strategies. Moreover, the analysts discovered that more challenging openings are played at lower levels and that players with lower ratings are more likely to engage in highly defensive opening strategies. Furthermore, the results indicate that chess players tend to imitate one another, particularly with regards to "Aggressive" opening strategies. Ultimately, the findings suggest that chess players are drawn to opening strategies characterized by long-range development and royal safety. 

